---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Vanguard: Self-Service Business Intelligence • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["Apple InfoSec", "Vanguard", "Lead Product Designer"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Tushar Roy // Case Study // Vanguard</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>Making Security Work Measurable</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				The InfoSec organization at Apple struggled to answer one
				question: <em>"How are we keeping Apple safe?"</em>.
			</p>
			<p>
				I led design for a <strong
					>self-service analytics platform</strong
				> that answered it though coverage and on-time commentary on metrics.
				The platform simplified metric management and paired AI-generated
				insights with human validation. Security leaders used it to plan efforts,
				investments, and priorities with confidence.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-vanguard/vg1_homepage.png"
					alt="Vanguard Homepage Interface - Executive Security Health Overview"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				Vanguard: Central executive dashboard displaying governed
				security metrics with live trust tiers and anomaly alerts.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Product Designer</h4>
				<p>Strategy, interaction model, governance design.</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"
					><time>2025 Q3</time> - <time>2026 Q2</time></small
				>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team</small>
				<h4>Core &amp; Partner Teams</h4>
				<p>
					1 Designer (me), 4 Developers, 2 Data engineers, 1 PM, 1
					SME, partner security orgs.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>30% → 75%</h4>
				<p>
					Escalation calls during incidents cut by more than half.
					Onboarding dropped from 2 months → 5 days.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: CONTEXT                                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">context</small>
				<h2>Vanguard Business Intelligence (BI)</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The BI team's charter was to publish performance metrics for
					senior leadership and partner teams to help make strategic,
					forward-looking decisions.
				</p>
				<p>
					They closely worked with each partner team to hand-build
					metrics - defining the measure, negotiating the formula,
					working with multiple data engineering teams, publishing,
					and handling ongoing maintenance.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong
						>Metrics published in Vanguard were the single source of
						trusted truth for Apple leadership.</strong
					>
				</p>

				<!-- Images: Side by Side -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg2_mttd_metric.png"
								alt="Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) Metric View"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Single Metric Detail View (MTTD)
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg2_programs.png"
								alt="Vanguard Security Programs Inventory"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Vanguard Security Programs Catalog
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Early Vanguard metrics and program structures were manually
					maintained by a central BI squad.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: THE PROBLEM                                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">problem</small>
				<h2>
					Executives had no defensible way to answer ‘How are we
					keeping Apple safe?’
				</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The small BI team could only cover <strong
						>~30% of the security teams</strong
					>. Others ended up creating their own dashboards to share
					with leadership directly. Executives were struggling for one
					holistic view they could trust.
				</p>

				<!-- Design Goals Card -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow">My Design Goals:</small>
					<ul>
						<li>
							<strong>Increase BI coverage</strong> - through simplified
							or faster onboarding.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong
								>Design and implement a holistic view for
								leadership</strong
							> - using a top-down results map.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Improve the platform interface</strong> - to better
							the dashboards of non-BI teams.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<!-- Images: Results Map & Incident Management -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-12 gap-5 pt-2">
					<div class="sm:col-span-7">
						<figure
							class="m-0 h-full flex flex-col justify-between"
						>
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-vanguard/vg3_results_map.png"
									alt="Top-down Results Map for Leadership"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								Holistic leadership view: mapping strategic
								goals to measures
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>

					<div class="sm:col-span-5 flex flex-col gap-5">
						<figure class="m-0 flex-1">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-vanguard/vg3_impact_metrics.png"
									alt="Impact Metrics Breakdown"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								High-level security impact metrics
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<figure class="m-0 flex-1">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-vanguard/vg3_incident_management.png"
									alt="Incident Management Dashboard"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								Non-BI team dashboard integration
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Connecting top-level strategic security objectives to
					team-level operational metrics.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: RESEARCH                                   -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">research</small>
				<h2>Metrics went stale, and anomalies unexplained</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I started with a <strong>design sprint</strong> with the BI team
					to align on goals and map how a metric actually gets made. Then
					I ran interviews on both ends of the pipe.
				</p>
				<p>
					I found onboarding was slow (<strong
						>average 2 months</strong
					>) primarily due to iteration between the two teams. Also,
					different teams had a variety of dashboards - using varied
					metrics across the board.
				</p>
				<p>
					My conversations with partner teams and executives uncovered
					two underlying challenges:
				</p>

				<!-- Two Core Challenges Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
					<div class="card">
						<h4>1. Published metrics went stale soon</h4>
						<p>
							Teams had to spend a lot of time continuously
							working with the BI team to maintain their metrics,
							and back-and-forth caused more friction than help.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<h4>2. Anomalies were displayed, but not explained</h4>
						<p>
							When a metric spiked, there was a flurry of
							questions, check-ins, and chaos as leadership wanted
							to understand the full picture.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Images: Research Discovery & Latency -->
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg5.png"
								alt="Research Findings: Onboarding Friction and Maintenance Choke Points"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Mapping the metric authoring lifecycle and
							identifying failure points
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg6.png"
								alt="Metric Workflow and Back-and-Forth Handoff Friction"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The 2-month handoff bottleneck between partner teams
							and central BI
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Visualizing the iterative bottlenecks that made central
					metrics lag operational realities.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: STRATEGY & REFRAMING                       -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">strategy</small>
				<h2>The team had two plans. I argued for one.</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>Main points from the Design Sprint Debate:</p>
				<small class="eyebrow">Director</small>
				<blockquote>
					"More BI headcount cannot solve the bottleneck problem,
					since teams are looking for autonomy and control."
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					<small class="eyebrow">Me, Director, Dev Lead</small>
					<blockquote>
						"Let's expand to a self-service platform, AI-powered
						anomaly detection and analysis."
					</blockquote>
				</p>
				<small class="eyebrow">Product Manager</small><blockquote>
					"That will dilute the trust factor we gained over years."
				</blockquote>
				<!-- Design Sprint Dialogue Box -->
				<div class="card">
					<h4>The Fundamental Question:</h4>
					<blockquote>
						"If anyone can publish, why would an executive believe
						anything?"
					</blockquote>
				</div>

				<div class="mt-8">
					<div class="card">
						<h4>Reframed Design Challenge:</h4>
						<blockquote>
							"How might we make governance and trust signals
							visible in a scalable business intelligence
							platform?"
						</blockquote>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Images: Strategic Architecture & Trust Signals -->
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg61.png"
								alt="Strategic Architecture: Governance and Trust Model"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Architectural framework for self-service with
							embedded governance
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg62.png"
								alt="Governing Trust Signals in a Scalable BI Platform"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Detailed interaction model mapping trust tiers
							directly into user workflows
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Strategy: Moving from gatekeeper reviews to visible,
					embedded provenance signals.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: DESIGN 1/4 - QUALITY GATES                 -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>1. Quality gates built into the onboarding flow</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Rather than reviewing quality after submission, I baked
					checks directly into the authoring path. The self-serve flow
					asks the same questions the BI team used to ask in review
					meetings:
				</p>

				<!-- 3 Checks Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CHECK 01</small>
						<h4>Formula Clarity</h4>
						<p>
							Is there an explicit, unambiguous formula for this
							measure?
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CHECK 02</small>
						<h4>Data Provenance</h4>
						<p>Are all contributing data sources declared?</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CHECK 03</small>
						<h4>Baseline Calibration</h4>
						<p>
							What does "good" look like, and against what
							baseline?
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Six Onboarding Steps: Side-by-Side Grid -->
				<div
					class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-2"
				>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_1.png"
								alt="Step 1: Formula definition and verification"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Measure — Partner teams define measures with
							clear formulas and data sources.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_2.png"
								alt="Step 2: Baseline targets and anomaly limits"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Thresholds — Baseline calibration sets
							statistical control limits and alert bands.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_3.png"
								alt="Step 3: Direct Responsible Individual (DRI) assignment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Ownership — Every metric requires a named DRI
							responsible for investigations.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_4.png"
								alt="Step 4: Continuous data pipeline validation"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							04. Automation — Continuous queries poll endpoints
							to ensure zero data drift.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_5.png"
								alt="Step 5: Change history and version logging"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							05. Audit Trail — Formula revisions and pipeline
							edits are immutably versioned.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg_contribute_6.png"
								alt="Step 6: Metric sign-off and publication"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							06. Certification — Automated verification qualifies
							the metric for live executive surfaces.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Steps 1 through 6 of the governed onboarding flow. Checks
					happen automatically as authors configure each metric.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: DESIGN 2/4 - TRUST TIERS & LINEAGE         -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>2. Always-attached trust tiers and data lineage</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					We started with Gold / Silver / Bronze states, but initial
					tests revealed <strong>attitudinal biases</strong> - lower tiers
					felt like something to hide, as opposed to a step to climb.
				</p>
				<p>
					We changed it to <strong
						>actual state which describes the provenance instead of
						status</strong
					>.
				</p>

				<!-- Provenance Trust Tiers Matrix Table -->
				<table class="m-0">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Tier</th>
							<th>Governance Meaning</th>
							<th>Author</th>
							<th>SLA</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><strong>BI Led</strong></td>
							<td>
								Defined, built, and maintained by the central BI
								team. Meets full enterprise standards.
							</td>
							<td>Central BI Squad</td>
							<td><code>99.9%</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><strong>BI Approved</strong></td>
							<td>
								Built by owning team, reviewed and signed off by
								BI. Quality gates verified.
							</td>
							<td>Partner Team + BI Review</td>
							<td><code>99.5%</code></td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><strong>Self-Service</strong></td>
							<td>
								Built and published autonomously by owning team.
								Clear lineage badge attached.
							</td>
							<td>Partner Team Autonomous</td>
							<td><code>Best Effort</code></td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>

				<!-- Images: Trust Tiers & Anomaly Data Lineage -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg8_trust_tiers.png"
								alt="Trust Tiers Interface showing BI-Led, BI-Approved, and Self-Service Badges"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Trust tier badges displayed contextually across
							dashboards
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg8_mttd_anomaly_data_lineage.png"
								alt="MTTD Anomaly Data Lineage Explorer"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Interactive data lineage explorer tracing raw
							telemetry sources
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Provenance, not status. Viewers evaluate confidence based on
					concrete data lineage rather than subjective rating.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: DESIGN 3/4 - HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP AI          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>3. Human-in-the-loop anomaly analysis</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					When a measure broke its control limits, an automated
					analysis was drafted by an agent and a notification sent to
					the directly responsible individual (DRI). Once edited and
					approved by the DRI, the analysis was available on the
					dashboard.
				</p>

				<!-- Trade-off Box -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow mb-2">
						AI Transparency vs Review Bottlenecks
					</small>
					<p>
						<strong>Design Trade-Off:</strong> Initially, the team wanted
						to hide automated analysis unless verified by a human. But
						the executives wanted to avoid another bottleneck of approval,
						and insisted on viewing AI analysis as well.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>Solution:</strong> We added a confidence score along
						with reasoning. Most importantly, I made it very clear visibly
						when somebody was looking at automated analysis.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Images: 01 Trigger (Full Width) + 02 Agent & 03 Sign-off (Side by Side) -->
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 pt-2">
					<!-- Full Width: 01. Trigger -->
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg9_mttd_anomaly.png"
								alt="MTTD Anomaly Detection Trigger"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Trigger — Metric breaks control limits; system
							flags anomaly immediately.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<!-- Side by Side: 02 & 03 -->
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
						<figure class="m-0">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-vanguard/vg9_mttd_anomaly_investigate.png"
									alt="AI Anomaly Investigation and Confidence Score"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								02. Agent — AI drafts narrative with explicit
								reasoning and confidence score.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<figure class="m-0">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-vanguard/vg9_mttd_anomaly_sign.png"
									alt="DRI Sign-Off and Executive Note Publication"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								03. Sign-off — DRI validates hypothesis and signs
								off before publishing.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Human-in-the-loop lifecycle: Autonomous detection →
					Transparent AI analysis → DRI human sign-off.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 9: DESIGN 4/4 - THE EXECUTIVE SURFACE         -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>4. The executive surface</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The strategy was to have a single yardstick for every
					measure. We used a <strong
						>statistical process control (SPC) chart</strong
					>, so we are able to distinguish normal variation from a
					genuine signal.
				</p>
				<p>
					This made it easy for executives to avoid reacting to false
					positives like <em>"we are off our average this week"</em>.
				</p>

				<!-- Images: [vg1_homepage.png] [vg7_homepage_critical_services.png] -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-vanguard/vg1_homepage.png"
								alt="Vanguard Executive Surface Overview"
